<Project>

  <!--
    ModularShop — hybrid referencing: the swap logic (the ONLY copy of it).

    A client solution normally consumes ModularShop as NuGet packages. A developer who also works ON
    ModularShop can opt in, on their own machine, to consuming it as PROJECT references instead — so a
    source edit shows up in the client on the next build, with no pack / push / version bump.

    This file performs that swap. It is imported by a client repo's committed `Directory.Build.targets`,
    and ONLY when that developer has opted in — so a package-only consumer (and CI, and production) never
    reads it and never needs a ModularShop clone at all.

    WHY THE LOGIC LIVES HERE, AND WHY CLIENTS IMPORT IT INSTEAD OF OWNING A COPY

      This logic could have lived in three other places. Each was rejected for a concrete reason.

      1. As a Choose/When/Otherwise block inside every client .csproj.
         This works, but every client project then holds its own copy of two things that change over
         time: the list of modules, and the paths into this repo's src/ folders. Across several client
         solutions those copies drift apart, and adding a module means hunting down and editing all of
         them. Written here instead, both exist exactly once.

      2. As this whole file, copied into each client repo.
         Better, but the module list and the paths are still duplicated once per repo, so they still
         drift. Because clients IMPORT this file rather than copy it, all a client repo needs is a stub
         that mentions no module and no path — and therefore never needs editing again.

      3. Shipped inside the ModularShop NuGet packages, so clients would get it automatically.
         The tidiest-sounding option, and the only one that is outright impossible:
           - NuGet forbids it. A package's build/*.props|targets may not add or remove PackageReference
             items; NuGet strips such items automatically.
           - It would arrive too late anyway. Those files reach a project through {project}.nuget.g.props,
             a file that restore itself generates — so they do not yet exist during the very restore that
             would need them.
           - It is circular. Switching to project references means REMOVING the ModularShop packages, so
             the logic would be deleting the package it was shipped inside.
         The full version of this argument is in docs/packaging-and-distribution.md §10.5.

      Two things follow from importing this file out of a local clone:

      - Package-only clients are completely unaffected. The client stub imports this file ONLY when a
        developer has opted in, so a client that has not opted in never reads it and needs no ModularShop
        clone. The promise that a client "runs with no ModularShop source anywhere" still holds.
      - The paths in the map below cannot go stale. They always describe the commit you currently have
        checked out, because they travel with it. A copy published elsewhere would freeze one layout and
        break silently the next time src/ is reorganised.

    WHY IT IS A `.targets` AND NOT A `.props`
      Directory.Build.props is imported BEFORE the .csproj body, when no PackageReference items exist yet —
      nothing to read, nothing to remove. Directory.Build.targets is imported AFTER the body, when they all
      exist. It is still EVALUATION time (an import, not a target), which is what NuGet restore reads.

    Input:
      $(ModularShopRepoRoot) — absolute path to the ModularShop repo root. The developer sets it in
      {Client}/ModularShop.local.props; there is no default anywhere, because no committed file should
      guess where an individual keeps their clone. By the time this file is imported the client stub has
      already checked that the path is set and really points at a ModularShop clone, so the map below can
      use it directly.
  -->

  <!--
    1. THE MAP: public package id -> the project file that produces it.

    This lists everything ModularShop publishes as a *public* surface — the five meta-packages plus the two
    Contracts packages (docs/packaging-and-distribution.md §1). It is deliberately NOT per-client: the
    intersection in step 2 narrows it down to whatever each project actually references, so the same file
    serves every client solution unchanged.

    ADDING A MODULE? Add one line here. That is the whole maintenance story.

    `ProjectPath` is custom metadata (not an MSBuild keyword) — an arbitrary named value carried on each
    item and read back in step 3 as %(ProjectPath). Paths are relative to $(ModularShopRepoRoot).
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Kernel hosting meta-package — the one every client host installs. -->
    <MSProjectMap Include="ModularShop.Kernel.Hosting"
                  ProjectPath="src/Kernel/ModularShop.Kernel.Hosting/ModularShop.Kernel.Hosting.csproj" />

    <!-- Feature module meta-packages — one per business capability a client can pick. -->
    <MSProjectMap Include="ModularShop.Modules.Sales"
                  ProjectPath="src/Modules/Sales/ModularShop.Modules.Sales/ModularShop.Modules.Sales.csproj" />
    <MSProjectMap Include="ModularShop.Modules.Warehouse"
                  ProjectPath="src/Modules/Warehouse/ModularShop.Modules.Warehouse/ModularShop.Modules.Warehouse.csproj" />
    <MSProjectMap Include="ModularShop.Modules.Shipping"
                  ProjectPath="src/Modules/Shipping/ModularShop.Modules.Shipping/ModularShop.Modules.Shipping.csproj" />
    <MSProjectMap Include="ModularShop.Modules.Support"
                  ProjectPath="src/Modules/Support/ModularShop.Modules.Support/ModularShop.Modules.Support.csproj" />

    <!-- Contracts packages — only referenced directly when a client compiles against a module's public
         surface (e.g. handling OrderPlaced itself). Mapped so those clients switch cleanly too. -->
    <MSProjectMap Include="ModularShop.Modules.Sales.Contracts"
                  ProjectPath="src/Modules/Sales/ModularShop.Modules.Sales.Contracts/ModularShop.Modules.Sales.Contracts.csproj" />
    <MSProjectMap Include="ModularShop.Modules.Warehouse.Contracts"
                  ProjectPath="src/Modules/Warehouse/ModularShop.Modules.Warehouse.Contracts/ModularShop.Modules.Warehouse.Contracts.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    2. NARROW THE MAP TO WHAT *THIS* PROJECT ACTUALLY REFERENCES.

    Evaluated once per project, so OrderingHub.Infrastructure (Kernel.Hosting + Sales + Warehouse) and
    OrderingHub.Host (Kernel.Hosting only) each get exactly their own set. Modules that a project never asked
    for are dropped automatically — nobody maintains a per-client list.

    This is set intersection expressed as two subtractions:

        Map        = { Kernel.Hosting, Sales, Warehouse, Shipping, Support, ...Contracts }
        PackageRef = { Kernel.Hosting, Sales, Warehouse, EFCore.SqlServer, Swashbuckle }

        _MSUnused  = Map minus PackageRef  = { Shipping, Support, ...Contracts }
        _MSSwap    = Map minus _MSUnused   = { Kernel.Hosting, Sales, Warehouse }   <- the intersection

    `Exclude` matches on item identity — package ids on both sides, so they line up.

    Why start from the map rather than from @(PackageReference)? Only the map carries the ProjectPath
    metadata. Starting from the map means the survivors arrive still knowing where their source lives.

    Why not a Condition that filters per item? Per-item %(...) batching in a Condition only works inside a
    <Target>, and a target runs too late for restore to see the result (see the header note). `Exclude` is
    one of the few filtering tools available during evaluation.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <_MSUnused Include="@(MSProjectMap)" Exclude="@(PackageReference)" />
    <_MSSwap   Include="@(MSProjectMap)" Exclude="@(_MSUnused)" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    3. THE SWAP.

    "Remove" drops those ids from the PackageReference list the .csproj built up (possible only because this
    file is imported after the body). The transform @(list->'pattern') then rewrites each surviving item
    into an absolute path to its .csproj, substituting that item's own %(ProjectPath).

    One line per module is still enough: ProjectReference is transitive in SDK-style projects exactly as
    PackageReference is, so referencing the Sales meta-PROJECT pulls Kernel.Hosting + Sales.Infrastructure,
    which pull Application -> Contracts/Domain/Api. The meta-projects' IncludeBuildOutput=false only
    affects `dotnet pack`; it has no effect on `dotnet build`, so referencing them here works normally.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Remove="@(_MSSwap)" />
    <ProjectReference Include="@(_MSSwap->'$(ModularShopRepoRoot)/%(ProjectPath)')" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    4. SAFETY NET: a ModularShop package that this file does not know about.

    If a client references a ModularShop package missing from the map above,
    it stays a PackageReference while its siblings became ProjectReferences — meaning the same
    assembly could arrive from two places. Warn instead of letting that surface as a confusing type or
    file-conflict error.

    This one IS a <Target>, because it only prints a diagnostic — it manipulates no items, so running at
    execution time is fine, and inside a target per-item %(...) batching is available.
  -->
  <Target Name="_MSWarnUnmappedModularShopPackages" BeforeTargets="Build">
    <Warning Condition="$([System.String]::Copy('%(PackageReference.Identity)').StartsWith('ModularShop.'))"
             Text="ModularShop project references are active, but '%(PackageReference.Identity)' is still a PackageReference because it is not in the map in ModularShop.ProjectReferences.targets. You may end up with two copies of the same assembly. Add it to the map, or drop the reference." />
  </Target>

</Project>
